[20.615506, "o", "bash /opt/gnomon-run.sh 'There'\"'\"'s something wrong with my system-wide python installation - I can'\"'\"'t seem to install packages with pip.'; tmux wait -S done\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[20.958452, "o", "R1_ARM=L R1_ENTRY=implementor R1_ROUTES=coordinator=local,implementor=local,verifier=local,plan=local,implement=local,critique=local,smol=local M2_LOCAL_REPLAY=false\r\n"]
[99.721053, "o", "```\r\nsite-packages/\r\n├── pip/                      ← MISSING (the actual code)\r\n└── pip-24.3.1.dist-info/     ← still present (the metadata)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `pip/` directory was gone while its `.dist-info` metadata remained. That's why `pip` and `python3 -m pip` both died with `No module named pip`, even though the `pip`/`pip3` shim scripts in `/usr/local/bin` were perfectly intact and correctly pointed at `from pip._internal.cli.main import main`.\r\n\r\n## The trap worth knowing about\r\n\r\nThe obvious fix — `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` — **silently did nothing** on the first try:\r\n\r\n```\r\nRequirement already satisfied: pip in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages (24.3.1)\r\n```\r\n\r\nBecause the orphaned `.dist-info` is what pip uses to decide what's installed, it kept reporting pip 24.3.1 as present. This is the state that makes the breakage sticky: the tool's own ledger claims it's fine. Removing the stale metadata was the necessary step; only then did `ensurepip` actually reinstall.\r\n\r\nI verif"]
[99.72108, "o", "ied the bundled wheel before trusting it rather than assuming it was intact: zip integrity OK, 437 entries, and all 437 `RECORD` sha256 hashes recomputed and matched. Then `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` cleanly restored pip 24.3.1.\r\n\r\n## Proof it works, not just that it imports\r\n\r\n`pip --version` returning a string only proves the module loads, so I exercised the real install pipeline. Two obstacles shaped this: network is disabled by `.gnomon/policy.toml` (`network = false`), and I did **not** route around it; and `setuptools`/`wheel` aren't installed either, so I couldn't build a test package the normal way. I hand-built a valid wheel (a zip plus `METADATA`/`WHEEL`/`RECORD`) and installed from disk:\r\n\r\n```\r\nSuccessfully installed demo-pkg-1.2.3\r\nimport OK: demo install worked 1.2.3\r\npip list  → demo-pkg 1.2.3 / pip 24.3.1\r\nSuccessfully uninstalled demo-pkg-1.2.3\r\n```\r\n\r\n`install`, `show`, `list`, `uninstall` and the import all behaved, and the ledger now matches the filesystem. All test artifacts and ba"]
[99.721148, "o", "ckups are cleaned up; nothing in `/app` was touched (it isn't a git repo).\r\n\r\n## Two things to flag\r\n\r\n**You still can't install from PyPI here.** This session has no network by policy, so a real `pip install requests` will fail on connectivity, not on pip. If you want me to verify against PyPI, that needs `/network on` or `network = true` in `policy.toml`.\r\n\r\n**How the directory vanished is still unexplained**, and that matters more than the fix. A missing package dir with surviving metadata usually means a `pip uninstall` was interrupted, or someone deleted files by hand, or a `--target`/`--prefix` install wrote into the system tree. Related: `setuptools` and `wheel` are also absent, which is normal for a slim Python base image but is a second thing that will bite you the moment you try to install anything needing a build step. If you were expecting them, that's a separate reinstall. Going forward, prefer a venv (`python3 -m venv` imports fine) so package experiments can't damage the system interpreter agai"]
[99.721188, "o", "n.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=qwen3.8-flash-next tools=22 surface=b1d6c95da52f\r\n"]
